{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775350189-crMzGgKj0pIAvbFmivDd","role":"assistant","created":1775350201,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the structure better. The tables show monthly data for the second quarter (January, February, March) for each fiscal year.\n\nThe question asks about the \"Employment and General Retirement net budget receipts\" growth from:\n- The month when FY2013 budget proposal was released\n- The month when FY2023 budget proposal was supposed to be released\n\nI need to identify:\n1. When the FY2013 budget proposal was released (which month)\n2. When the FY2023 budget proposal was supposed to be released (which month)\n\nThe FY (fiscal year) runs from October 1 to September 30. So:\n- FY2013 would be October 2012 to September 2013\n- FY2023 would be October 2022 to September 2023\n\nBut I need to figure out when the budget proposal was released. Usually, the President's budget is released in early February. For FY2013, the budget was released in February 2012. For FY2023, the budget was released in March 2022 (since it was delayed from February).\n\nWait, let me think about this more carefully. The question is asking about \"the month that the FY2013 budget proposal was released\" and \"the month that the FY2023 budget proposal was supposed to be released.\"\n\nThe budget for FY2013 was released in February 2012. Looking at the data, we have \"Employment and general retirement\" values for January, February, and March of each year:\n\nFor FY2012 (the 2012_06 bulletin), we have:\n- Jan: 74.8\n- Feb: 60.6  \n- Mar: 69.3\n\nWait, but the table shows \"Second-Quarter Net Budget Receipts by Source, Fiscal Year 2012\" - so this is for FY2012, which runs from Oct 2011 to Sep 2012.\n\nThe FY2013 budget proposal would have been released in February 2012 (for the fiscal year starting October 2012).\n\n\nI notice the table header confirms the data is for the second quarter of fiscal year 2012, which complicates my initial understanding. I'll carefully cross-reference the bulletin dates to ensure accuracy for fiscal years 2013 and 2023. The FY2013 budget proposal was released in February 2012, and the FY2023 proposal was supposed to be released in February 2022. However, I realize the June 2022 bulletin actually displays data for the second quarter of FY2022, which spans January to March 2022. This means I'm examining monthly data within those specific fiscal years, not the budget proposal release months. The timing and interpretation require careful attention to the precise fiscal year definitions.\n\nI'll trace the fiscal year progression carefully. Budget proposals are typically released in the winter preceding the fiscal year's start. For FY2013, this means the proposal came out in February 2012, while for FY2023, it was expected in February 2022. Since the fiscal year begins in October, these budget proposals actually precede the fiscal year's commencement.\n\nI'm examining treasury bulletins for June 2012 and June 2022, which provide monthly details for those fiscal years. The second quarter data reveals interesting patterns in Employment and General Retirement net budget receipts, showing potential variations in financial reporting.\n\nAfter verifying the fiscal year details, I confirm February 2012 and February 2022 are the relevant months. The Employment and General Retirement figures for those periods - 60.6 and 104.6 billion dollars respectively - suggest significant changes in budget allocations.\n\nI'll proceed with calculating the percentage change between these two time points, carefully verifying each step to ensure accuracy in my financial analysis.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_bb23b23d8a24d25c","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /app/resources/*.json"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
